The '''2022 Hamilton West by-election''' was a [[List of New Zealand by-elections|by-election]] in New Zealand's {{NZ electorate link|Hamilton West}} electorate. The seat became vacant due to the resignation of former [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]] MP [[Gaurav Sharma (politician)|Gaurav Sharma]] on 18 October 2022 after expulsion from the party caucus.<ref name=resigned>{{Cite news |date=20 October 2022 |title=Embattled MP Gaurav Sharma says he's resigned, triggering by-election |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2022/10/18/embattled-mp-gaurav-sharma-says-hes-resigned-triggering-by-election/ |work=[[1News]] |access-date=20 October 2022}}</ref>

The by-election was held on 10 December 2022, with twelve candidates on the ballot, and was won by the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]] candidate [[Tama Potaka]]. The results were reported by media outlets and opposition parties as a sign of waning support for Prime Minister [[Jacinda Ardern]]'s administration and the Labour Party ahead of the [[2023 New Zealand general election]], although the by-election was marked by low turnout.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.scmp.com/news/asia/australasia/article/3202867/jacinda-arderns-2023-chances-dented-labour-loses-new-zealand-election|title=Jacinda Ardern's 2023 chances dented as Labour loses New Zealand by-election|publisher=South China Morning Post|via=Bloomberg News|date=11 December 2022|accessdate=1 January 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/480500/opposition-tout-political-change-for-2023-elections-after-hamilton-west-seat-win|title=Opposition tout political change for 2023 elections after Hamilton West seat win|publisher=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=11 December 2022|accessdate=1 January 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.smh.com.au/world/oceania/staring-into-a-looming-recession-ardern-shrugs-off-by-election-defeat-20221212-p5c5m7.html|title=Staring into a looming recession, Ardern shrugs off byelection defeat|publisher=[[The Sydney Morning Herald]] |last=McKay|first=Ben|date=12 December 2022|accessdate=1 January 2023}}</ref>

==Background==
In August 2022, Sharma was first suspended and then expelled from the Labour parliamentary caucus after breaching confidentiality of caucus communications. On 18 October 2022, Sharma resigned from Parliament, claiming that Labour leader [[Jacinda Ardern]] was planning to wait until the [[2023 New Zealand general election]] was less than six months away and then invoke the [[Electoral (Integrity) Amendment Act 2018]], also known as [[waka-jumping]] legislation. This would vacate Sharma's Hamilton West seat late enough in the 53rd Parliament to avoid a by-election, leaving the electorate unrepresented. Ardern, [[Grant Robertson]] and [[Chris Hipkins]] rejected this unproven claim.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/476899/gaurav-sharma-resigns-as-an-mp-months-after-expulsion-from-labour |title=Gaurav Sharma resigns as an MP months after expulsion from Labour |publisher=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=18 October 2022 |accessdate=20 October 2022}}</ref> On 20 October, Ardern announced that the by-election would be held on 10 December.<ref>{{Cite news |title=PM Jacinda Ardern says Hamilton West by-election will be held on December 10; Labour Party expels Gaurav Sharma |first1=Bridie |last1=Witton |first2=Glenn |last2=McConnell |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300716919/pm-jacinda-ardern-says-hamilton-west-byelection-will-be-held-on-december-10-labour-party-expels-gaurav-sharma |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=20 October 2022 |access-date=20 October 2022 }}</ref>

Ardern was critical of Sharma's decision to resign and trigger a by-election, saying it was a waste of taxpayer money.<ref>{{Cite news |title=Gaurav Sharma has left Parliament, but did he really need to? |first=Glenn |last=McConnell |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/130222437/gaurav-sharma-has-left-parliament-but-did-he-really-need-to |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=19 October 2022 |access-date=20 October 2022 }}</ref>

=== Electorate ===
Hamilton West covers the western half of [[Hamilton, New Zealand|Hamilton]], New Zealand's fourth-largest city. It covers the entire [[Hamilton City Council (New Zealand)|Hamilton City Council]] area west of the [[Waikato River]], as well as part of [[Flagstaff, Hamilton|Flagstaff]] east of the river.<ref name=":0">{{cite web |date=27 April 2021 |title=Hamilton West – Electorate Profile |url=https://www.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/electorate-profiles/hamilton-west-electorate-profile/ |access-date=19 October 2022 |publisher=[[New Zealand Parliament]]}}</ref>

This was Hamilton West's first by-election. The electorate is regarded as a [[Bellwether (politics)|bellwether seat]]; in all but one general election since the electorate's creation in 1969, the party that has won the plurality of seats nationally has won Hamilton West.<ref name=":0" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Sowman-Lund |first=Stewart |date=4 November 2022 |title=Very steep uphill battle for Sharma in Hamilton West – poll |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/live-updates/04-11-2022/very-steep-uphill-battle-for-sharma-in-hamilton-west-poll |access-date=3 November 2022 |website=[[The Spinoff]] }}</ref>

