Resigned MP [[Gaurav Sharma (politician)|Gaurav Sharma]] contested the by-election under the banner of his new New Zealand Momentum Party.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/hamilton-west-byelection-gaurav-sharma-claims-raid-of-labour-manpower-as-new-party-revealed/TIAX6Y3OGRAGDN5P2VDKRRHXDQ/ |title=Hamilton West byelection: Gaurav Sharma claims raid of Labour manpower as new party revealed |date=4 November 2022 |work=[[New Zealand Herald]] |first=Adam |last=Pearse}}</ref> His campaign was backed financially by wealthy businessman Roshan Nauhria who was involved with the now defunct [[New Zealand People's Party|People's Party]].<ref>{{Cite news |title=Wealthy businessman Roshan Nauhria willing to financially back Gaurav Sharma |first=Jenna |last=Lynch |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/10/wealthy-businessman-roshan-nauhria-willing-to-financially-back-gaurav-sharma.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221019221852/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/10/wealthy-businessman-roshan-nauhria-willing-to-financially-back-gaurav-sharma.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=19 October 2022 |work=[[Newshub]] |date=19 October 2022 |access-date=20 October 2022 }}</ref> Sharma had stated that he intended to form a new centrist party "with focus on outcomes and action rather than on ideologies."<ref name=resigned/>

The [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]] candidate was Georgie Dansey ([[Ngāti Tūwharetoa]]), the chief executive of the [[Independent Schools Education Association]]. In 2020 she sought the Hamilton West nomination and was 81st on Labour's [[Party_lists_in_the_2020_New_Zealand_general_election#Labour_Party |party list]].<ref>{{Cite news |title=Labour selects Georgie Dansey to run in Hamilton West by-election |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/130337901/labour-selects-georgie-dansey-to-run-in-hamilton-west-byelection |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=1 November 2022 |access-date=1 November 2022 }}</ref><ref name="Pearse-1Nov">{{cite news |last1=Pearse |first1=Adam |title=Labour selects Georgie Dansey as Hamilton West byelection candidate |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/labour-selects-georgie-dansey-as-hamilton-west-byelection-candidate/5SW2IPXBXVD4LAJ7OTZIDCUXG4/ |access-date=1 November 2022 |publisher=[[New Zealand Herald]] |date=1 November 2022}}</ref><ref name="diversity">{{cite web |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/hamilton-west-byelection-whos-lining-up-for-national-and-labour-and-will-it-answer-nationals-diversity-problem/HWSO63AGKOAPC6LXQSDSZDL6HU/ |title=Hamilton West byelection: Who's lining up for National and Labour – and will it answer National's diversity problem? |date=24 October 2022 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |first=Claire |last=Trevett |url-access=subscription }}</ref> Others who had been seen as possible Labour candidates included Dan Steer, an electorate staffer and candidate in the [[Hamilton City Council]]'s 2022 local elections, and city councillor Maxine van Oosten, who declined to seek nomination citing commitments as the council's Finance and Monitoring Committee chairperson.<ref name="step up">{{Cite news |title=Who will step up in Hamilton West after Gaurav Sharma's resignation? |first=Rachel |last=Moore |url= https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300715406/who-will-step-up-in-hamilton-west-after-gaurav-sharmas-resignation |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=19 October 2022 |access-date=20 October 2022 }}</ref>

The [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]] candidate was Tama Potaka, chief executive of [[Ngāi Tai ki Tāmaki]].<ref>{{Cite news |title=Tama Potaka announced as National's pick to contest Hamilton West by-election |first=Jonah |last=Franke-Bowell |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/300732155/tama-potaka-announced-as-nationals-pick-to-contest-hamilton-west-byelection |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=6 November 2022 |access-date=6 November 2022 }}</ref> Two other potential candidates were shortlisted – health manager Dr Frances Hughes, and Rachel Afeaki-Taumoepeau (a business director and former chair of the New Zealand Tonga Business Council who had been on [[Party_lists_in_the_2020_New_Zealand_general_election#National_Party|the party's list in 2020]]).<ref name="Trevett-31Oct">{{cite news |last1=Trevett |first1=Claire |title=National Party shortlist for Hamilton West byelection announced |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/national-candidates-for-hamiton-west-byelection-announced/RP5E2EEJTBGULMQNQX5HM3ZR3Y/ |access-date=31 October 2022 |publisher=[[New Zealand Herald]] |date=31 October 2022}}</ref><ref name="Franke-1Nov">{{cite news |last1=Franke |first1=Jonah |title='Underdog' National Party unveils diverse candidate shortlist for Hamilton West by-election |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/300726857/underdog-national-party-unveils-diverse-candidate-shortlist-for-hamilton-west-byelection |access-date=1 November 2022 |publisher=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=1 November 2022}}</ref> In the lead-up to the selection, party leader [[Christopher Luxon]] repeatedly committed to making the party's caucus more diverse.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Pearse |first1=Adam |title=Hamilton West byelection: National sets candidate expectations amid diversity dilemma |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/hamilton-west-byelection-national-sets-candidate-expectations-amid-diversity-dilemma/ELMI5SKBD2LCEMZY6IUUIUBAMM/ |access-date=26 October 2022 |publisher=[[New Zealand Herald]] |date=25 October 2022}}</ref> Both former MP [[Tim Macindoe]], who lost the Hamilton West seat to Sharma in 2020, and former one-term Hamilton mayor [[Andrew King (mayor)|Andrew King]] had indicated an interest in the candidacy before ultimately standing aside.<ref>{{cite news |title=Former National MP won't stand in Hamilton West by-election |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2022/10/26/former-national-mp-wont-stand-in-hamilton-west-by-election/ |access-date=26 October 2022 |publisher=1 News |date=26 October 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Leaman |first1=Aaron |title=Return of the King: former Hamilton mayor eyes seat in the Beehive |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/waikato-times/news/128493090/return-of-the-king-former-hamilton-mayor-eyes-seat-in-the-beehive |access-date=31 October 2022 |publisher=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=30 April 2022}}</ref><ref name="step up"/><ref>{{cite news |last1=Pearse |first1=Adam |title=Hamilton West byelection: Former mayor Andrew King out of National's candidate race |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/hamilton-west-byelection-former-mayor-andrew-king-out-of-nationals-candidate-race/VOKPKAPHCVEQXMPTVJUYCDQFXI/ |access-date=31 October 2022 |publisher=[[New Zealand Herald]] |date=31 October 2022}}</ref><ref name="surprise exit">{{Cite news |title=Hamilton West byelection: Gaurav Sharma's surprise exit hurts Labour's chances – Jacinda Ardern |first=Adam |last=Pearse |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/hamilton-west-byelection-gaurav-sharmas-surprise-exit-hurts-labours-chances-jacinda-ardern/EDZEK4CHCYHPJA6ZPRC6QYTV5M/ |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=19 October 2022 |access-date=20 October 2022 }}</ref> ''[[The New Zealand Herald]]'''s political editor, Claire Trevett, consequently wrote that the party had effectively implemented a "white-man ban".<ref name="white-man ban">{{cite web |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/claire-trevett-oh-the-diversity-national-leader-christopher-luxon-and-the-white-man-ban-in-hamilton-west/IS3OY3XMQBA2XK2T5ZFFQIDTMU/ |title=Claire Trevett: Oh, the diversity! National leader Christopher Luxon and the 'white-man ban' in Hamilton West |date=1 November 2022 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |first=Claire |last=Trevett |url-access=subscription }}</ref>

[[The Opportunities Party]]'s (TOP's) candidate was Naomi Pocock, a project manager at the [[University of Waikato]] and TOP's candidate in [[Hamilton East (New Zealand electorate)|Hamilton East]] at the 2020 election, where she placed fourth.<ref>{{cite web |first=Jamie |last=Ensor |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/10/hamilton-west-by-election-the-opportunities-party-puts-forward-naomi-pocock.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221027225738/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/10/hamilton-west-by-election-the-opportunities-party-puts-forward-naomi-pocock.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=27 October 2022 |title=Hamilton West by-election: The Opportunities Party puts forward Naomi Pocock |date=28 October 2022 |work=[[Newshub]] |access-date=28 October 2022 }}</ref> TOP's 2020 candidate for Hamilton West, Hayden Cargo, had moved to Auckland and taken up the role of Party Secretary.<ref name="surprise exit"/> 

[[ACT New Zealand]]'s candidate was list MP [[James McDowall]].<ref name="ACT and Wakeman">{{cite web |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/478001/act-chooses-sitting-mp-to-join-hamilton-by-election-race |title=ACT chooses sitting MP to join Hamilton by-election race |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=3 November 2022 }}</ref> He won 5.2% of the [[Waikato (New Zealand electorate)|Waikato electorate]] vote in 2020 and 0.39% of Hamilton East's vote in 2017.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ensor |first1=Jamie |title=Hamilton West by-election: ACT Party announces Dr James McDowall as candidate |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/11/hamilton-west-by-election-act-party-announces-dr-james-mcdowall-as-candidate.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221103052407/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/11/hamilton-west-by-election-act-party-announces-dr-james-mcdowall-as-candidate.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=3 November 2022 |access-date=7 November 2022 |publisher=[[Newshub]] |date=3 November 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Hamilton East: Electoral Profile |url=https://www.parliament.nz/mi/mps-and-electorates/historical-electorate-profiles/electorate-profiles-data/document/DBHOH_Lib_EP_Hamilton_East_Electoral_Profile/hamilton-east-electoral-profile |website=New Zealand Parliament (Pāremata Aotearoa) |access-date=7 November 2022}}</ref>

The [[New Conservative Party (New Zealand)|New Conservative Party]] and [[ONE Party]] cooperated, with Rudi du Plooy as their joint candidate.<ref name="franke-bowell-7nov">{{cite news |last1=Franke-Bowell |first1=Jonah |title=One candidate, two parties: Conservative coalition picks Hamilton West contender |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/waikato-times/news/300732811/one-candidate-two-parties-conservative-coalition-picks-hamilton-west-contender |access-date=7 November 2022 |publisher=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=7 November 2022}}</ref> In 2020, du Plooy stood in the same seat for the New Conservatives, while in 2019 and 2022 he ran unsuccessfully for a West Ward seat on the Hamilton City Council.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Cooke |first1=Henry |title=New Conservative candidate shares digitally edited billboard while complaining of vandalism, says another party pranked him |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300069823/new-conservative-candidate-shares-digitally-edited-billboard-while-complaining-of-vandalism-says-another-party-pranked-him |publisher=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |access-date=7 November 2022 |date=7 November 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Hamilton City Council candidates confirmed |url=https://hamilton.govt.nz/your-council/news/community-environment/hamilton-city-council-candidates-confirmed |website=Hamilton City Council |access-date=7 November 2022 |date=17 August 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Bathgate |first1=Benn |last2=Rolleston |first2=Te Aorewa |title='Revenge' and anti-vax agendas – the motives behind Hamilton's mayoral hopefuls |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/waikato-times/news/300675179/revenge-and-antivax-agendas--the-motives-behind-hamiltons-mayoral-hopefuls |access-date=7 November 2022 |publisher=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=12 September 2022 |quote=Rudi Du Plooy is a member of a four-strong Team Integrity group seeking election to Hamilton City Council.}}</ref>

[[NZ Outdoors & Freedom Party]] co-leader [[Donna Pokere-Phillips]] also stood.<ref name="franke-bowell-7nov" /> She previously ran for both the Hamilton mayoralty and a seat in the city's new Māori Ward in October 2022, and in the city council's 2021 Hamilton East by-election. In national politics she stood in [[Hauraki-Waikato]] for the [[Māori Party]] in 2020 and in Hamilton West for [[The Opportunities Party]] in 2017.

The [[Aotearoa Legalise Cannabis Party]] selected pilot, and non-cannabis user, Peter Wakeman as their candidate. A [[perennial candidate]], Wakeman had recently stood at the [[2022 Christchurch mayoral election]].<ref name="ACT and Wakeman"/>

[[Vision NZ]], under the [[far-right]] umbrella party [[Freedoms NZ]], were represented by Jade Tait.<ref>{{cite web |title=Vision NZ announces candidate for Hamilton West |url=http://www.voxy.co.nz/politics/5/409467 |website=Voxy |access-date=8 November 2022 |date=8 November 2022}}</ref> Her father, Derek Tait, is a [[Destiny Church (New Zealand)|Destiny Church]] pastor and a leader within the [[The Freedoms & Rights Coalition|Freedom & Rights Coalition]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Van Beynen |first1=Martin |title=Derek Tait: Destiny Church's mouth in the south |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/127480081/derek-tait-destiny-churchs-mouth-in-the-south |access-date=8 November 2022 |publisher=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=15 January 2022|quote=His daughter Jade owns a $700,000 house in Woodend with her partner.}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://theconversation.com/refusing-to-rule-out-working-with-brian-tamaki-luxon-gives-nzs-populist-right-a-sniff-of-credibility-189368 |title=Refusing to rule out working with Brian Tamaki, Luxon gives NZ’s populist right a ‘sniff of credibility’ |date=26 August 2022 |work=The Conversation |first=Richard |last=Shaw}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/brian-tamakis-umbrella-party-to-contest-hamilton-west-byelection/DRFB6JTPONQD2FRWU4DGRIX3IE/ |title=Brian Tamaki’s umbrella party to contest Hamilton West byelection |date=24 October 2022 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |first=Akula |last=Sharma }}</ref>

The [[Green Party of Aotearoa New Zealand |Green Party]] and [[New Zealand First]] did not contest the election.<ref name="Pearse-1Nov" /><ref>{{cite news |title=NZ First will not contest Hamilton West byelection, says Winston Peters |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/live-updates/02-11-2022/nz-first-will-not-contest-hamilton-west-byelection-says-winston-peters |access-date=2 November 2022 |publisher=[[The Spinoff]] }}</ref>

==Campaigning==
The regulated period for election expenses began on Thursday, 20 October. Two weeks of advance voting started on Monday 28 November.<ref name="NZParl">{{cite web |title=Learn more about the Hamilton West by-election |url=https://www.parliament.nz/en/get-involved/features/learn-more-about-the-hamilton-west-by-election/ |access-date=23 November 2022 |website=New Zealand Parliament (Pāremata Aotearoa)}}</ref> Television and radio advertising was allowed only from Wednesday, 2 November to Friday, 9 December, when all forms of advertising were required to end by midnight.<ref name="timetable">{{cite web |title=2022 Hamilton West by-election timetable |url=https://vote.nz/2022-hamilton-west-by-election/about/overview/by-election-timetable/ |website=vote.nz |publisher=Electoral Commission New Zealand}}</ref> A spending limit of $61,100 (including GST) applied to each candidate.<ref>{{cite web |title=2022 Hamilton West by-election: Rules for campaigning, expenses and donations |url=https://vote.nz/2022-hamilton-west-by-election/about/overview/rules-for-campaigning-expenses-and-donations/ |website=vote.nz |access-date=1 November 2022}}</ref>

Both Labour and National claimed "underdog" status even before their candidates had been selected.<ref name="Franke-1Nov" /><ref>{{cite news |title=Sharma: Ardern says Labour 'underdog' in Hamilton West seat |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/national/programmes/checkpoint/audio/2018863360/sharma-ardern-says-labour-underdog-in-hamilton-west-seat |access-date=1 November 2022 |publisher=[[RNZ]] |date=19 October 2022 |quote=Jacinda Ardern says her party is the "underdog" in the Hamilton West electorate.}}</ref>

The day after her candidacy was announced, Labour's Georgie Dansey was seen at an ambush protest against her own party's health minister, [[Andrew Little (New Zealand politician)|Andrew Little]]. The protest, at the [[University of Waikato]], was organised by the [[Tertiary Education Union]] and focused on the working conditions of University staff. Little had visited the University to make an announcement about student mental health.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Franke-Bowell |first1=Jonah |title=Labour Hamilton West candidate seen at ambush protest against own minister |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/300728543/labour-hamilton-west-candidate-seen-at-ambush-protest-against-own-minister |access-date=2 November 2022 |date=2 November 2022}}</ref> In a subsequent [[Facebook]] post on 2 November 2022, Dansey denied being part of the ambush protest, saying "I was at the uni today in my capacity as an education sector union rep. I wasn't there to protest the Minister and when it became clear the Minister was being ambushed, I left".<ref>{{Cite web |date=3 November 2022 |title=Labour Hamilton West by-election candidate Georgie Dansey denies protesting Labour minister Andrew Little day after selection |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/11/labour-hamilton-west-by-election-candidate-georgie-dansey-denies-protesting-labour-minister-andrew-little-day-after-selection.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221102195847/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/11/labour-hamilton-west-by-election-candidate-georgie-dansey-denies-protesting-labour-minister-andrew-little-day-after-selection.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=2 November 2022 |access-date=3 November 2022 |website=Newshub.}}</ref>

On 5 November Sharma was ordered by [[New Zealand Parliamentary Service|Parliamentary Service]] chief executive Rafael Gonzalez-Montero to remove a sign outside his former electorate office stating he was the MP for Hamilton West, which was incorrect after his resignation. Sharma stated he had not received any order and the matter was "...up to Parliamentary Service to sort it out."<ref>{{Cite web |first=Jenna |last=Lynch |title=Hamilton West by-election: Gaurav Sharma ordered to remove sign saying he's MP for Hamilton West |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/11/hamilton-west-by-election-gaurav-sharma-ordered-to-remove-sign-saying-he-s-mp-for-hamilton-west.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221105094402/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/11/hamilton-west-by-election-gaurav-sharma-ordered-to-remove-sign-saying-he-s-mp-for-hamilton-west.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=5 November 2022 |website=[[Newshub]] |date=5 November 2022 |access-date=5 November 2022 }}</ref>

On 17 November, Luxon announced National's youth crime policies while campaigning with Hamilton West candidate Tama Potaka, Police spokesperson [[Mark Mitchell (New Zealand politician)|Mark Mitchell]], and justice spokesperson [[Paul Goldsmith (politician)|Paul Goldsmith]]. Key policy announcements included the creation of a new Young Serious Offender category and [[Boot camps in New Zealand|boot camp]]s known as "Youth Offender Military Academies" for juvenile offenders, [[electronic monitoring]], targeting gangs, and funding community groups.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Palmer |first1=Russell |title=Youth crime boot camps: National proposes military academies, electronic monitoring |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/478917/youth-crime-boot-camps-national-proposes-military-academies-electronic-monitoring |access-date=6 September 2023 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=17 November 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221118082324/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/478917/youth-crime-boot-camps-national-proposes-military-academies-electronic-monitoring |archive-date=18 November 2022|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Stuff boot camps">{{cite news |last1=Franke-Bowell |first1=Jonah |title=National would bring back military boot camps for young offenders |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300740657/national-would-bring-back-military-boot-camps-for-young-offenders |access-date=6 September 2023 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=17 November 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221119180551/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300740657/national-would-bring-back-military-boot-camps-for-young-offenders |archive-date=19 November 2022|url-status=live}}</ref> During the policy announcement, Potaka said that Hamilton had become known as the "[[Ram-raiding|ramraid]] capital of New Zealand" in reference to the nationwide surge in ram raids in 2022. He also stated that he wanted "Hamilton West to be the best city to grow up and grow old in."<ref name="Stuff boot camps" />

==Opinion polling==
A poll was conducted by Curia and commissioned by the ACT Party during 21 and 22 October, before any candidates other than Sharma had been announced and before NZ First announced it would not contest the election. The poll asked 400 eligible voters, "while candidates for the by-election haven't been announced yet, which party's candidate are you most likely to vote for?" It reported that 44.7% of respondents said they were most likely to vote for the National Party candidate, compared with 36.6% for a Labour candidate, 8.6% for the ACT candidate, 4.7% for the Green candidate, and 2.9% for the NZ First candidate. Only 2.5% answered the question saying they would most likely vote for Sharma.<ref name="poll">{{cite web |last=Lynch |first=Jenna |date=3 November 2022 |title=Hamilton West by-election: Internal party poll results revealed as Dr Gaurav Sharma levels accusation at Labour candidate Georgie Dansey |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/11/exclusive-hamilton-west-poll-results-revealed-as-dr-gaurav-sharma-levels-accusation-at-labour-candidate-georgie-dansey.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221103055106/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/11/exclusive-hamilton-west-poll-results-revealed-as-dr-gaurav-sharma-levels-accusation-at-labour-candidate-georgie-dansey.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=3 November 2022 |work=[[Newshub]] }}</ref> 

A second poll, the first after candidates were announced, was released on 5 December. National's Tama Potaka was on 46% of decided voters, Labour's Georgie Dansey with 33%, ACT's [[James McDowall]] on 12% and Sharma on 4%.<ref name="poll2">{{cite web |last=Franke-Bowell |first=Jonah |date=5 December 2022 |title=National holds double-digit edge over Labour in Hamilton West by-election poll |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300756731/national-holds-doubledigit-edge-over-labour-in-hamilton-west-byelection-poll |website=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] }}</ref>

